summary:
The four kernels should stay separate build-time products. Building them into one binary that picks its mode at boot costs too much:
- kern_small inside kern_big would cost +5,440 bytes of tables (task stacks 960 -> 2,816, FAT window 1,024 -> 4,608). kern_small has 5,120 bytes of headroom.
- kern_mega inside kern_big would put roughly 12-14.7KB of protected-mode code on every XT. kern_big has 4,096 bytes of headroom.

Windows 3.0 made the same choice: three kernel binaries from shared source, with WIN.COM picking one at start-up. Much of that model is already here and works well:
- build names that imply each other and refuse bad combinations;
- a set of feature switches resolved once, above the includes;
- about 400 macro sites that serve real and protected mode without separate code paths;
- packages that run unchanged on kern_big and kern_small.

What is missing is a check that keeps each kernel's separate code honest. Of the review's 64 code bugs, 32 live in code that only one kernel or kernel group carries: 8 kern_mega, 4 kern_small, 20 the kern_big feature set. Only 5 of those are true divergence, where one contract has two diverging implementations:
- two kern_big bugs fixed only on kern_mega (drivers-3, hiber-2);
- three kern_small refusal stubs or guards (abi-2, dw-8, video-1).

Test coverage runs inverse to importance. kern_small has 28 soak rows; kern_mega has about 430; test-full never boots kern_mega. t_hotpar pins entry parity on kern_big and kern_small but not on kern_mega, even though every kern_mega machine has the 16-bit bus where parity matters.

The near model still limits kern_big at the VGA rung's 64KB window: 6,946 bytes left, not the 19,842 that the KERN_CODE_MAX figure suggests. It does not limit kern_mega (14,056 free) or kern_small (32,565 free). Inside the kernel it is now a placement rule.

Recommended now, with no ABI break and only a few resident bytes:
- the one-kernel fixes plus a rule (gkm-1);
- abi-3's four-kernel ABI gate plus refusal stubs that name their contract (gkm-2);
- a MartyPC smallport.txt list (gkm-3);
- t_hotpar on kern_mega plus a self-correcting parity macro (gkm-4);
- a ratchet on raw build-name tests (gkm-7);
- retire kern_emu by rebuilding VMMOUSE.DRV the way USBMOUSE.DRV works (gkm-6, about +40 bytes on kern_big).

Recorded rather than recommended:
- do not merge the kernels at run time (gkm-5);
- do not flatten kern_small to one code segment while its ROM is a product (gkm-8, -540 bytes);
- one system disk for the 8088 and the 286 only after L3 (gkm-9).

The overall line: kernels may fork, packages should not. L3 is the package half of the matrix question.
